 ## Background
-The concept of optimizing voxel data for volume rendering and simulation has evolved alongside real-time graphics capabilities. Early methods focused on binary occupancy, but modern applications require multi-channel data (density, albedo, normals). The "Volume Buffer" concept, introduced in 2011, demonstrated how GPU-based rasterization could be adapted for 3D volume accumulation using layered rendering extensions [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments, such as those seen in projects like VoxelConeTracingMetal, have integrated bounding volume optimizations and projection culling to handle dynamic scenes efficiently [S2, unofficial]. The field continues to expand with sparse data structures and hierarchical acceleration structures like LBVH to manage large-scale voxel datasets [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of voxelization as a bridge between polygonal models and volume representations has been fundamental in computer graphics for applications ranging from smoke/fluid simulations to global illumination [S1, unofficial]. Early methods focused on simple surface voxelization algorithms that were easy to implement and integrate into existing frameworks [S1, unofficial]. The introduction of GPU-based techniques allowed for real-time generation of volume data from arbitrary complex and dynamic models [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments introduced optimizations such as the Volume Buffer for multi-channel data accumulation [S1, unofficial] and various culling and compression strategies to handle the increasing complexity of scenes [S2, unofficial].
 
